  <metadata>
    <id>shep</id>
    <!-- Not the same string as <id>, per the package validator's CPMR0050. -->
    <title>shep Process Manager</title>
    <!-- Replaced at pack time: the workflow passes the version off the
         release tag to `choco pack`. The literal here never ships. -->
    <version>0.0.0</version>
    <authors>TurtIeSocks</authors>
    <owners>TurtIeSocks</owners>
    <projectUrl>https://shep-pm.com</projectUrl>
    <projectSourceUrl>https://github.com/shep-pm/shep</projectSourceUrl>
    <packageSourceUrl>https://github.com/shep-pm/shep/tree/main/packaging/chocolatey</packageSourceUrl>
    <docsUrl>https://shep-pm.com/docs/getting-started</docsUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>https://github.com/shep-pm/shep/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <licenseUrl>https://github.com/shep-pm/shep#license</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <!-- web/public/favicon.svg, padded to a square and put on the site's
         meadow green. The source is web/public/icon.svg and the PNG beside it
         is rasterised from that at 256x256. Served from shep-pm.com rather
         than from raw.githubusercontent.com, which CPMR0076 forbids: the site
         is GitHub Pages behind a CDN, and the file is in web/public/ so an
         Astro build copies it to the site root. -->
    <iconUrl>https://shep-pm.com/icon.png</iconUrl>
    <copyright>Copyright (c) TurtIeSocks</copyright>
    <!-- Spaces, never commas (CPMR0014), and no "chocolatey" (CPMR0048). -->
    <tags>shep process-manager supervisor daemon cli rust pm2 foreground service</tags>
    <summary>A process manager that keeps a flock of long-running processes alive, with logs, watch and cron restarts, and webhook alerts</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[
shep is a process manager written in Rust. One binary runs a daemon called the
shepherd, which keeps a flock of your long-running processes alive, restarts
them when they die, captures what they print, and says plainly when something
is wrong.

Every command answers with the whole flock, not just the process you touched.
The face in the STATUS column is the fastest thing to read: `(o.o)` online,
`(o~o)` starting, `(>_<)` waiting to restart, `(-.-)` stopped, `(x.x)` errored.

## What it does

* Supervises long-running processes and restarts them when they exit
* Captures stdout and stderr to rotating log files, readable with `shep bleats`
* Restarts on file changes, on a cron schedule, or over a memory limit
* A terminal dashboard, `shep lookout`
* Webhook alerts, an MCP server, and a key-value store the flock can read

## Three things it will not do on Windows

Windows is the newest of the three supported platforms and these gaps are
real rather than temporary omissions:

* `shep startup` is not built. Boot-time supervision on Windows means a
  Service Control Manager service, which is a different program shape rather
  than another unit template. Run `shep start` in your own session, or wrap
  `shep runtime` in NSSM or WinSW.
* There is nothing SIGTERM-shaped that can be delivered to an arbitrary
  process, so `shep stop` waits out the process's whole `kill_timeout` before
  terminating it. An app that opts into the shepherd channel with
  `shutdown_with_message` gets a graceful stop instead.
* The `user` and `group` fields refuse permanently. Dropping privilege on
  Windows needs a logon session or a primary-token privilege, which is a
  different feature rather than a different call.

## Notes on this package

Only `shep.exe` is shimmed. The archive also carries `shep-runtime.exe` and
`shep-dev.exe`, which are container entrypoint aliases with no desktop use
case, so they are installed without shims.

`choco uninstall` refuses while a shepherd is still running, rather than
stopping your processes on your behalf. Run `shep kill` first.
]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/shep-pm/shep/releases</releaseNotes>
    <!-- shep.exe imports VCRUNTIME140.dll, which ships with the Visual C++
         redistributable rather than with Windows. Rust's MSVC targets link the
         CRT dynamically unless the build sets `+crt-static`, and this one does
         not, so a machine without the redistributable fails to start the
         binary at all. Verified by reading the import strings out of the
         shep-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.zip that shep-v0.1.25 published. -->
    <dependencies>
      <dependency id="vcredist140" />
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
  </files>
</package>
